
Here’s a modified version of your paragraph, maintaining your writing style while adding a bit of a personal touch:

In Night by Elie Wiesel, Elie relies on his faith and his bond with his father to navigate the unimaginable horrors of the Holocaust. In one particularly harrowing scene, Elie is on the brink of giving up as he trudges through a brutal winter march. Yet, it’s the presence of his father that pushes him to keep going. Wiesel captures this struggle when he writes, “My father’s presence was the only thing that stopped me. … I had no right to let myself die. What would he do without me?” (Wiesel 86). Here, Elie’s profound sense of responsibility toward his father propels him forward, even amid exhaustion and despair. This illustrates how the bond between Elie and his father serves as a crucial source of strength, intertwining their survival.

Later in the memoir, Elie grapples with his faith and how it shapes his will to live. In a moment of deep anguish, he contemplates surrendering but clings to the fading threads of his belief in God, even as he begins to question it. Wiesel writes, “I was not denying His existence, but I doubted His absolute justice” (Wiesel 45). This doubt signals a crisis of faith, yet it paradoxically fuels his desire to keep fighting. The struggle with belief becomes a catalyst for Elie, forcing him to confront his despair while holding on to a glimmer of hope. Through these moments, Wiesel illustrates how Elie’s connection to his father and his evolving faith provide essential support in his relentless fight for survival.

